<section image-layout="blank" id="E4YIz13r6Y" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item><h2>Freedom takes institutional form—and institutions remain revisable</h2><p font-size="18pt">Freedom is not simply doing whatever one wants. Agency becomes effective through shared practices and durable institutions, while participants can also criticize and reshape those forms.</p><freeform-block w="840" h="190"><box type="content" x="0" y="14" w="230" h="160" sizing="fixed" background-color="surface" style="padding:16pt 18pt; border-radius:4pt;"><p role="eyebrow">Person</p><h3>Individual agency</h3><p font-size="18pt">I form purposes, make commitments, and act.</p></box><shape preset="rightArrow" x="240" y="55" w="55" h="20" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="240" y="111" w="55" h="20" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0" flip-h="true"></shape><box type="content" x="305" y="14" w="230" h="160" sizing="fixed" background-color="surface" style="padding:16pt 18pt; border-radius:4pt;"><p role="eyebrow">Practice</p><h3>Shared norms</h3><p font-size="18pt">Speaking, listening, challenge, and responsibility make agency effective.</p></box><shape preset="rightArrow" x="545" y="55" w="55" h="20" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="rightArrow" x="545" y="111" w="55" h="20" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0" flip-h="true"></shape><box type="content" x="610" y="14" w="230" h="160" sizing="fixed" background-color="surface" style="padding:16pt 18pt; border-radius:4pt;"><p role="eyebrow">Institution</p><h3>Durable social form</h3><p font-size="18pt">Roles, rights, procedures, and avenues for revision organize freedom over time.</p></box></freeform-block><blockquote><p font-size="18pt"><b>No automatic progress.</b> Institutions can embody freedom inadequately; their conflicts can expose reasons for criticism and change.</p></blockquote><p role="caption">Scoped teaching summary. Source: <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hegel”</a></p></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Emphasize both directions of the arrows: practices and institutions enable action, but acting participants can also contest and revise them. Do not present historical development as guaranteed improvement.</p></notes></section>